@page "/docs/cli/license"

@* The template license in plain language: a summary of LICENSE-TEMPLATES at the repository's root (the IzzyBlazor
   Template License; the private catalog's LICENSE is the same text). The license decides, so change this page
   whenever its text changes. *@

<ArticlePage Section="cli" Slug="license">
    <Lead>
        The code izzy creates is yours to use in any app. The components and the <Code>izzy</Code> command are MIT
        licensed. The starters, blocks and kits that come with an IzzyBlazor account have their own license, the
        IzzyBlazor Template License. This page explains that license in plain language.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">What each license covers</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>MIT:</strong> the components, the <Code>izzy</Code> command, the IzzyBlazor App starter, and the
                grid page from <Code>izzy add crud</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>IzzyBlazor Template License:</strong> the starters, blocks and kits that come with an account,
                free or Pro.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            The terms are the same for a free account and for Pro. Pro adds more templates.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What you can do</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Code that izzy creates from a template with your account is yours to use. You can:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Use it in any number of apps, sites and services, whether personal, internal or commercial.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Use it in work for a client, and give the app's code to the client.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Change it in any way.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Ship it with your app, as source code or compiled.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            You don't need to credit IzzyBlazor, and the files need no license header.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What you can't do</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                Offer the code, changed or not, as a template, starter, kit, block, theme or boilerplate for other
                people to build their apps from.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>Put the code in a component library, a template collection, a design kit or a code generator.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Share the templates themselves. Only the code izzy creates from them is yours to pass on.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                Share your account or license key, or create code for someone who needs their own account to create it.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Teams</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each person who creates code with <Code>izzy</Code> or an IzzyBlazor editor plugin needs their own account.
            Teammates who only edit that code don't need one.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Open-source projects</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            You can publish an app that contains the code in a public repository. The repository can't itself be a
            template or a starter for other people's apps.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">When your account ends</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Code you created while you had the account stays yours, under the same terms. This is true when a Pro
            subscription ends too.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The full text</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            This page is a summary. The
            <Anchor Href="https://github.com/enabledevmode/izzyblazor/blob/main/LICENSE-TEMPLATES">IzzyBlazor Template License</Anchor>
            is the agreement, and it decides wherever the two differ. Like MIT-licensed code, the templates and the
            code created from them come without a warranty.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

